Managing living animals requires a deep understanding of animal physiology and inheritance patterns .
Poultry Breeding Cycles: Studying recessive and dominant inheritance patterns (Punnett squares) in poultry feather colors or egg-laying metrics; maintaining controlled incubator variables (relative humidity, heat) .
Veterinary Care Logs: Administering basic animal medical treatments, studying parasite lifecycles under a microscope, and keeping meticulous health records including dosage-by-weight calculations .
Agrarian History & Farm Accounting (Core Social Studies)
To understand modern economics, students must analyze the historical forces that shaped human communities .
Ozark Agrarian History: Studying the economic and cultural history of Southwest Missouri communities; comparing 19th-century subsistence homesteading with modern commercial farming .
Supply Chain Analysis: Mapping the origin and transportation logistics of animal feed, seeds, and farm supplies; investigating how regional grain markets and global supply chains affect local feed prices .
Farm Enterprise Accounting: Setting up double-entry ledger systems for egg sales, vegetable boxes, or livestock transactions; calculating profit margins, return on investment (ROI), and asset depreciation .
